<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-parametrization" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Parametrization of Plane Curves</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Describe the motion of a point in the plane with a pair of parametric
equations <m>x = f(t)</m> and <m>y = g(t)</m>, and understand the
parameter <m>t</m> as recording <em>when</em> the point visits each place
on the curve.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Sketch a parametric curve from a table of values, and indicate the
direction of increasing <m>t</m> in which the curve is traced.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find parametric equations for common curves, such as lines, circles, and
ellipses, and eliminate the parameter to recover a Cartesian equation
when possible.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Model the motion of a projectile with parametric equations, and derive
the equation of its parabolic trajectory.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Distinguish the <term>intersection points</term> of two parametric curves
from their <term>collision points</term>, where two moving objects reach
the same place at the same time.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-parametric-equations">
<title>Parametric Equations</title>

<p>
Imagine that you would like to describe the motion of an object whose
motion is confined to a plane. If you know how its Cartesian
coordinates, <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, change as time passes, you will be
able to draw a curve that represents the trajectory of its motion, as
shown in <xref ref="fig-parametric-motion-video"/>. Mathematically, we
will need two equations to demonstrate how the <m>x</m>- and
<m>y</m>-coordinates evolve in time <m>t</m>, which are known as the
<term>parametric equations</term>.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-parametric-equations">
<title>Parametric Equations</title>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are given as functions of a third variable
<m>t</m>, called a <term>parameter</term>, by
<md xml:id="eq-parametric-equations" number="yes">
x = f(t), \qquad y = g(t),
</md>
then the points <m>(x,y) = (f(t),\,g(t))</m> trace out a curve in the
plane, called a <term>parametric curve</term>. The equations
<m>x=f(t)</m> and <m>y=g(t)</m> are the parametric equations of the
curve.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<figure xml:id="fig-parametric-motion-video">
<caption>
A point moves in the plane. As time <m>t</m> passes, its coordinates
<m>x = f(t)</m> and <m>y = g(t)</m> change, and the point traces out
the trajectory of the motion.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-parametric-motion"
       source="videos/parametric_motion_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-parametric-motion">
<caption>
The trajectory of a moving point whose coordinates are given by the
parametric equations <m>x = f(t)</m>, <m>y = g(t)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-parametric-motion">
<diagram dimensions="(360,300)" margins="10">
<definition>f(t)=(0.62*t*cos(t), 0.62*t*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-7,-4.5,6,7)">
<grid-axes at="pm-grid" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="pm-curve" function="f" domain="(0.4, 9.6)"
                  stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
<point at="pm-start" p="f(0.4)" fill="red"/>
<point at="pm-end" p="f(9.6)" fill="red"/>
<label at="pm-eq-label" anchor="(-6.7,6.2)" alignment="e" color="blue">
<m>(x,y)=(f(t),\,g(t))</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate grid showing a spiral curve, the trajectory of a moving point whose coordinates are given by the parametric equations x equals f of t and y equals g of t. The curve starts near the origin and spirals outward.">
  <annotation ref="pm-grid" text="A coordinate grid with x and y axes."/>
  <annotation ref="pm-curve" text="The trajectory of the motion, a spiral drawn in blue that starts near the origin and winds outward."/>
  <annotation ref="pm-start" text="The starting position of the moving point, near the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="pm-end" text="The final position of the moving point, at the outer end of the spiral."/>
  <annotation ref="pm-eq-label" text="The label x comma y equals f of t comma g of t, indicating that each point of the curve is given by the parametric equations."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
As an example, consider <m>x = t</m> and <m>y = t^2</m>. Let us
calculate some of the points <m>(x,y)</m>.
</p>

<table xml:id="table-parabola-points">
<title>Points on the curve <m>x=t</m>, <m>y=t^2</m></title>
<tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
<row header="yes" bottom="major">
<cell><m>t</m></cell>
<cell><m>x</m></cell>
<cell><m>y</m></cell>
</row>
<row><cell><m>-3</m></cell><cell><m>-3</m></cell><cell><m>9</m></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>-2</m></cell><cell><m>-2</m></cell><cell><m>4</m></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>4</m></cell></row>
<row><cell><m>3</m></cell><cell><m>3</m></cell><cell><m>9</m></cell></row>
</tabular>
</table>

<p>
Plotting these points and connecting them, we recognize the parabola
<m>y = x^2</m>, traced from left to right as <m>t</m> increases from
<m>-3</m> to <m>3</m>. See <xref ref="fig-parabola-param-video"/> and
<xref ref="fig-parabola-param"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-parabola-param-video">
<caption>
The parametric curve <m>x = t</m>, <m>y = t^2</m>: the points from the
table appear first, and then the moving point traces out the parabola
<m>y = x^2</m> in the direction of increasing <m>t</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-parabola-param"
       source="videos/parabola_param_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-parabola-param">
<caption>
The curve <m>x = t</m>, <m>y = t^2</m> is the parabola <m>y = x^2</m>.
The marked points correspond to the integer values
<m>t = -3, \dots, 3</m> from the table, and the arrows show the
direction of increasing <m>t</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-parabola-param">
<diagram dimensions="(340,320)" margins="10">
<definition>g(t)=(t, t^2)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-4.9,-1,4.9,9.8)">
<grid-axes at="pb-grid" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="pb-curve" function="g" domain="(-3,3)"
                  stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
<point at="pb-p1" p="(-3,9)" fill="red"/>
<point at="pb-p2" p="(-2,4)" fill="red"/>
<point at="pb-p3" p="(-1,1)" fill="red"/>
<point at="pb-p4" p="(0,0)" fill="red"/>
<point at="pb-p5" p="(1,1)" fill="red"/>
<point at="pb-p6" p="(2,4)" fill="red"/>
<point at="pb-p7" p="(3,9)" fill="red"/>
<vector at="pb-dir-left" v="(0.3,-1.5)" tail="(-2.6,6.76)" stroke="blue"/>
<vector at="pb-dir-right" v="(0.3,1.4)" tail="(2.3,5.29)" stroke="blue"/>
<label at="pb-t-start" anchor="(-3,9)" alignment="w" color="red">
<m>t=-3</m>
</label>
<label at="pb-t-end" anchor="(3,9)" alignment="e" color="red">
<m>t=3</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate grid showing the parabola y equals x squared, traced by the parametric equations x equals t and y equals t squared. Seven points corresponding to integer values of t from negative 3 to 3 are marked in red, and arrows on the curve show the direction of increasing t.">
  <annotation ref="pb-grid" text="A coordinate grid with x and y axes."/>
  <annotation ref="pb-curve" text="The parabola y equals x squared, drawn in blue for t between negative 3 and 3."/>
  <annotation ref="pb-p1" text="The point negative 3 comma 9, corresponding to t equals negative 3."/>
  <annotation ref="pb-p2" text="The point negative 2 comma 4, corresponding to t equals negative 2."/>
  <annotation ref="pb-p3" text="The point negative 1 comma 1, corresponding to t equals negative 1."/>
  <annotation ref="pb-p4" text="The origin, corresponding to t equals 0."/>
  <annotation ref="pb-p5" text="The point 1 comma 1, corresponding to t equals 1."/>
  <annotation ref="pb-p6" text="The point 2 comma 4, corresponding to t equals 2."/>
  <annotation ref="pb-p7" text="The point 3 comma 9, corresponding to t equals 3."/>
  <annotation ref="pb-dir-left" text="An arrow on the left branch pointing down toward the origin, the direction of increasing t."/>
  <annotation ref="pb-dir-right" text="An arrow on the right branch pointing up and away from the origin, the direction of increasing t."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
As our second example, consider the equation of the circle centered at
<m>(0,0)</m> with unit radius,
<md xml:id="eq-unit-circle" number="yes">
x^2 + y^2 = 1.
</md>
</p>
<p>
Here, we would like to parametrize <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in terms of a
single parameter <m>t</m>, as in <xref ref="eq-parametric-equations"/>, in
such a way that by varying the value of <m>t</m> we can reconstruct the
circle <xref ref="eq-unit-circle"/>. Remember that
<m>\cos^2(t) + \sin^2(t) = 1</m>, so one way to achieve our goal is to
write
<md xml:id="eq-unit-circle-param" number="yes">
x = \cos(t), \qquad y = \sin(t),
</md>
where <m>t \in [0, 2\pi)</m>. The parametrization is illustrated in
<xref ref="fig-unit-circle-param-video"/> and
<xref ref="fig-unit-circle-param"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-unit-circle-param-video">
<caption>
Parametrizing the unit circle: as <m>t</m> increases from <m>0</m> to
<m>2\pi</m>, the point <m>(\cos(t), \sin(t))</m> travels
counterclockwise around the circle, passing through the marked points
at <m>t = 0</m>, <m>\pi/2</m>, <m>\pi</m>, and <m>3\pi/2</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-unit-circle-param"
       source="videos/unit_circle_param_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-unit-circle-param">
<caption>
Parametrizing the unit circle <m>x^2+y^2=1</m> by
<m>x=\cos(t)</m>, <m>y=\sin(t)</m>, <m>t \in [0,2\pi)</m>. A generic
point of the circle corresponds to the parameter value <m>t_0</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-unit-circle-param">
<diagram dimensions="(376,240)" margins="10">
<definition>c(t)=(cos(t), sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.5,-1.5,3.2,1.5)">
<grid-axes at="uc-grid" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="uc-curve" function="c" domain="(0, 2*pi)"
                  stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
<line at="uc-radius" endpoints="((0,0), c(pi/5))" stroke="green"/>
<point at="uc-t0-point" p="c(pi/5)" fill="green"/>
<point at="uc-mark-0" p="(1,0)" fill="red"/>
<point at="uc-mark-half-pi" p="(0,1)" fill="red"/>
<point at="uc-mark-pi" p="(-1,0)" fill="red"/>
<point at="uc-mark-3half-pi" p="(0,-1)" fill="red"/>
<label at="uc-label-0" anchor="(1,0)" alignment="se" color="red">
<m>t=0</m>
</label>
<label at="uc-label-half-pi" anchor="(0,1)" alignment="nw" color="red">
<m>t=\frac{\pi}{2}</m>
</label>
<label at="uc-label-pi" anchor="(-1,0)" alignment="sw" color="red">
<m>t=\pi</m>
</label>
<label at="uc-label-3half-pi" anchor="(0,-1)" alignment="se" color="red">
<m>t=\frac{3\pi}{2}</m>
</label>
<label at="uc-label-t0" anchor="c(pi/5)" alignment="ne" color="green">
<m>(x,y)=(\cos(t_0),\,\sin(t_0))</m>
</label>
<angle-marker at="uc-angle-t0" points="((1,0),(0,0),c(pi/5))" radius="20" clear-background="no">
<m>t_0</m>
</angle-marker>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The unit circle centered at the origin, parametrized by x equals cosine of t and y equals sine of t. Four points at t equals 0, pi over 2, pi, and 3 pi over 2 are marked in red, and a generic green point corresponds to the parameter value t naught.">
  <annotation ref="uc-grid" text="A coordinate grid with x and y axes."/>
  <annotation ref="uc-curve" text="The unit circle, drawn in blue, traced counterclockwise as t increases from 0 to 2 pi."/>
  <annotation ref="uc-radius" text="A green radius from the origin to the generic point of the circle at parameter value t naught."/>
  <annotation ref="uc-t0-point" text="A generic point of the circle, with coordinates cosine of t naught comma sine of t naught."/>
  <annotation ref="uc-mark-0" text="The point 1 comma 0, where t equals 0."/>
  <annotation ref="uc-mark-half-pi" text="The point 0 comma 1, where t equals pi over 2."/>
  <annotation ref="uc-mark-pi" text="The point negative 1 comma 0, where t equals pi."/>
  <annotation ref="uc-mark-3half-pi" text="The point 0 comma negative 1, where t equals 3 pi over 2."/>
  <annotation ref="uc-angle-t0" text="The angle t naught between the positive x axis and the radius to the generic point."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<exercise xml:id="question-another-parametrization">
<title>Question</title>
<statement>
<p>
Can you think of another way to write a parametric form of the circle
<m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>?
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-parametrizing-examples">
<title>Some Examples of Parametrizing Curves</title>

<example xml:id="ex-parametrizing-curves">
<title>Parametrizing Curves</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the parametric equations corresponding to the following curves.
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p>The line segment connecting the two points <m>(1,0)</m> and <m>(0,1)</m>.</p></li>
<li><p>The ellipse <m>\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \dfrac{y^2}{9} = 1</m>.</p></li>
<li><p>The circle of radius <m>2</m> centered at <m>(2,0)</m>.</p></li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<paragraphs xml:id="sol-parametrizing-curves">
<title>Solution</title>

<p>
<em>Part A.</em> The segment lies on the line <m>y = 1 - x</m>, so we
may take
<md xml:id="eq-segment-param" number="yes">
x = t, \qquad y = 1 - t, \qquad 0 \le t \le 1.
</md>
</p>
<p>
At <m>t = 0</m> we are at the point <m>(0,1)</m>, and at <m>t = 1</m>
we arrive at the point <m>(1,0)</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-segment-param-video"/> and
<xref ref="fig-segment-param"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-segment-param-video">
<caption>
The line segment from <m>(0,1)</m> to <m>(1,0)</m>, traced by
<m>x = t</m>, <m>y = 1-t</m> as <m>t</m> increases from <m>0</m> to
<m>1</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-segment-param"
       source="videos/segment_param_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-segment-param">
<caption>
Part A: the segment <m>x = t</m>, <m>y = 1-t</m>,
<m>0 \le t \le 1</m>, which starts at <m>(0,1)</m> when <m>t=0</m> and
ends at <m>(1,0)</m> when <m>t=1</m>.
</caption>
<image width="50%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-segment-param">
<diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(-0.3,-0.35,1.35,1.3)">
<grid-axes at="seg-grid" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<line at="seg-segment" endpoints="((0,1),(1,0))" stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
<point at="seg-start" p="(0,1)" fill="red"/>
<point at="seg-end" p="(1,0)" fill="red"/>
<label at="seg-label-start" anchor="(0,1)" alignment="ne" color="red">
<m>t=0</m>
</label>
<label at="seg-label-end" anchor="(1,0)" alignment="s" color="red">
<m>t=1</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate grid showing the line segment from the point 0 comma 1 to the point 1 comma 0, traced by the parametric equations x equals t and y equals 1 minus t for t between 0 and 1.">
  <annotation ref="seg-grid" text="A coordinate grid with x and y axes."/>
  <annotation ref="seg-segment" text="The line segment, drawn in blue, from 0 comma 1 down to 1 comma 0."/>
  <annotation ref="seg-start" text="The starting point 0 comma 1, where t equals 0."/>
  <annotation ref="seg-end" text="The ending point 1 comma 0, where t equals 1."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<em>Part B.</em> Guided by the unit circle, we take
<md xml:id="eq-ellipse-param" number="yes">
x = 2\cos(t), \qquad y = 3\sin(t), \qquad 0 \le t \lt 2\pi.
</md>
</p>
<p>
To verify, note that
<md>
\frac{(2\cos t)^2}{4} + \frac{(3\sin t)^2}{9}
= \cos^2 t + \sin^2 t = 1.
</md>
</p>
<p>
See <xref ref="fig-ellipse-param-video"/> and
<xref ref="fig-ellipse-param"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-ellipse-param-video">
<caption>
The ellipse <m>\frac{x^2}{4} + \frac{y^2}{9} = 1</m>, traced
counterclockwise by <m>x = 2\cos(t)</m>, <m>y = 3\sin(t)</m>, with the
points at <m>t = 0</m>, <m>\pi/2</m>, <m>\pi</m>, and <m>3\pi/2</m>
marked.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-ellipse-param"
       source="videos/ellipse_param_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-ellipse-param">
<caption>
Part B: the ellipse <m>x = 2\cos(t)</m>, <m>y = 3\sin(t)</m>,
<m>0 \le t \lt 2\pi</m>.
</caption>
<image width="45%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-ellipse-param">
<diagram dimensions="(300,320)" margins="10">
<definition>e(t)=(2*cos(t), 3*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.6,-3.9,3.6,3.9)">
<grid-axes at="el-grid" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="el-curve" function="e" domain="(0, 2*pi)"
                  stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
<point at="el-mark-0" p="(2,0)" fill="red"/>
<point at="el-mark-half-pi" p="(0,3)" fill="red"/>
<point at="el-mark-pi" p="(-2,0)" fill="red"/>
<point at="el-mark-3half-pi" p="(0,-3)" fill="red"/>
<label at="el-label-0" anchor="(2,0)" alignment="se" color="red">
<m>t=0</m>
</label>
<label at="el-label-half-pi" anchor="(0,3)" alignment="ne" color="red">
<m>t=\frac{\pi}{2}</m>
</label>
<label at="el-label-pi" anchor="(-2,0)" alignment="sw" color="red">
<m>t=\pi</m>
</label>
<label at="el-label-3half-pi" anchor="(0,-3)" alignment="se" color="red">
<m>t=\frac{3\pi}{2}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate grid showing an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal semi axis 2 and vertical semi axis 3, traced by x equals 2 cosine t and y equals 3 sine t. Four points at t equals 0, pi over 2, pi, and 3 pi over 2 are marked in red.">
  <annotation ref="el-grid" text="A coordinate grid with x and y axes."/>
  <annotation ref="el-curve" text="The ellipse, drawn in blue, traced counterclockwise as t increases from 0 to 2 pi."/>
  <annotation ref="el-mark-0" text="The point 2 comma 0, where t equals 0."/>
  <annotation ref="el-mark-half-pi" text="The point 0 comma 3, at the top of the ellipse, where t equals pi over 2."/>
  <annotation ref="el-mark-pi" text="The point negative 2 comma 0, where t equals pi."/>
  <annotation ref="el-mark-3half-pi" text="The point 0 comma negative 3, at the bottom of the ellipse, where t equals 3 pi over 2."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<em>Part C.</em> The equation of such a circle in Cartesian coordinates
is <m>(x-2)^2 + y^2 = 4</m>, so we shift the standard parametrization
of a circle of radius <m>2</m> by <m>2</m> units in the
<m>x</m>-direction:
<md xml:id="eq-shifted-circle-param" number="yes">
x = 2\cos(t) + 2, \qquad y = 2\sin(t), \qquad 0 \le t \lt 2\pi.
</md>
</p>
<p>
To verify, note that
<md>
\bigl((2\cos t + 2) - 2\bigr)^2 + (2\sin t)^2
= 4\cos^2 t + 4\sin^2 t = 4.
</md>
</p>
<p>
See <xref ref="fig-shifted-circle-param-video"/> and
<xref ref="fig-shifted-circle-param"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-shifted-circle-param-video">
<caption>
The circle of radius <m>2</m> centered at <m>(2,0)</m>, traced by
<m>x = 2\cos(t) + 2</m>, <m>y = 2\sin(t)</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-shifted-circle-param"
       source="videos/shifted_circle_param_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-shifted-circle-param">
<caption>
Part C: the circle <m>x = 2\cos(t)+2</m>, <m>y = 2\sin(t)</m>,
<m>0 \le t \lt 2\pi</m>, of radius <m>2</m> centered at <m>(2,0)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-shifted-circle-param">
<diagram dimensions="(340,280)" margins="10">
<definition>s(t)=(2*cos(t)+2, 2*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.8,-2.7,4.8,2.7)">
<grid-axes at="sc-grid" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="sc-curve" function="s" domain="(0, 2*pi)"
                  stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
<point at="sc-center" p="(2,0)" fill="green"/>
<point at="sc-mark-0" p="(4,0)" fill="red"/>
<point at="sc-mark-half-pi" p="(2,2)" fill="red"/>
<point at="sc-mark-pi" p="(0,0)" fill="red"/>
<point at="sc-mark-3half-pi" p="(2,-2)" fill="red"/>
<label at="sc-label-center" anchor="(2,0)" alignment="ne" color="green">
<m>(2,0)</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate grid showing a circle of radius 2 centered at the point 2 comma 0, traced by x equals 2 cosine t plus 2 and y equals 2 sine t. The center is marked in green, and the four points where t equals 0, pi over 2, pi, and 3 pi over 2 are marked in red.">
  <annotation ref="sc-grid" text="A coordinate grid with x and y axes."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-curve" text="The circle of radius 2 centered at 2 comma 0, drawn in blue, traced counterclockwise."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-center" text="The center of the circle, at the point 2 comma 0, marked in green."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-mark-0" text="The point 4 comma 0, where t equals 0."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-mark-half-pi" text="The point 2 comma 2, at the top of the circle, where t equals pi over 2."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-mark-pi" text="The origin, where t equals pi."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-mark-3half-pi" text="The point 2 comma negative 2, at the bottom of the circle, where t equals 3 pi over 2."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</paragraphs>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-projectile-motion">
<title>*Projectile Motion</title>

<p>
<em>Starred section.</em> This one is for the interested reader. It will not
be examined.
</p>

<p>
A type of parametric equation you may have already seen is the equation of motion for a
projectile. Here the parameter is time, and <m>(x(t), y(t))</m> gives the position of the
projectile at time <m>t</m>.
</p>

<p>
Recall two basic facts about motion in one dimension from your introductory physics course.
For motion along a straight line at constant speed <m>v</m>, the position is
<md xml:id="eq-motion-constant-speed" number="yes">x(t) = v\, t + x_0.</md>
For motion with uniform acceleration <m>a</m>, the position is
<md xml:id="eq-motion-uniform-accel" number="yes">x(t) = \frac{1}{2} a t^2 + v_0 t + x_0.</md>
</p>

<p>
Now consider an object moving under the influence of gravity alone, ignoring air resistance.
No horizontal force acts on it, so it moves at constant speed in the <m>x</m>-direction; in the
<m>y</m>-direction it moves with constant acceleration <m>g</m>, directed downward. Resolving
the initial velocity into its horizontal component <m>v_0 \cos(\theta)</m> and vertical
component <m>v_0 \sin(\theta)</m>, and applying
<xref ref="eq-motion-constant-speed"/> and
<xref ref="eq-motion-uniform-accel"/>, gives the equations of
motion for a projectile:
<md number="yes">
  <mrow xml:id="eq-projectile-x">x(t) \amp = v_0 \cos(\theta)\, t + x_0,</mrow>
  <mrow xml:id="eq-projectile-y">y(t) \amp = - \frac{1}{2} g t^2 + v_0 \sin(\theta)\, t + y_0,</mrow>
</md>
where <m>v_0</m> is the initial speed, <m>\theta</m> is the launch angle, and <m>g</m> is the
acceleration due to gravity. For simplicity, we take the initial position <m>(x_0, y_0)</m> to
be the origin <m>(0,0)</m>.
</p>

<p>
To find the trajectory of the projectile, we can solve for <m>t</m> and substitute into the <m>y(t)</m> equation <xref ref="eq-projectile-y"/>.
Solving for <m>t</m> in the <m>x(t)</m> equation <xref ref="eq-projectile-x"/> gives
<md number="yes">
<mrow xml:id="eq-projectile-t">t \amp = \frac{x}{v_0 \cos(\theta)}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
  Substituting <xref ref="eq-projectile-t"/> into the <m>y(t)</m> equation <xref ref="eq-projectile-y"/> gives
<md>
  <mrow number="no"> \amp y = - \frac{1}{2} g \frac{x^2}{v_0^2 \cos^2(\theta)} + v_0 \sin(\theta) \frac{x}{v_0 \cos(\theta)} </mrow>
  <mrow xml:id="eq-projectile-trajectory" number="yes"> \amp y = - \frac{g}{2 v_0^2 \cos^2(\theta)} x^2 + x \tan(\theta)  </mrow>
</md>

</p>

<p>
As expected, the trajectory equation <xref ref="eq-projectile-trajectory"/> shows that the trajectory of a projectile is a parabola. Also, note that differentiating the
equations of motion gives the velocity of the projectile: the horizontal
component <m>v_x = v_0\cos(\theta)</m> stays constant, since nothing accelerates
the projectile horizontally, while the vertical component
<m>v_y = v_0\sin(\theta) - g t</m> decreases steadily under gravity, so it is
positive while the projectile rises, zero at the apex, and negative as it falls.
<xref ref="fig-projectile-velocity"/> shows these components at five instants
along the path.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-projectile-velocity">
<caption>
The velocity of a projectile at five instants along its parabolic path.
The horizontal component <m>\overrightarrow{v}_x</m> stays constant while the vertical
component <m>\overrightarrow{v}_y</m> changes under the constant downward acceleration
<m>\overrightarrow{g}</m>; at the apex the vertical component is zero.
</caption>
<image width="90%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-projectile-velocity">
<diagram dimensions="(500,265)" margins="5">
  <definition>f(x) = 0.14*x*(10 - x)</definition>
  <coordinates bbox="(-1.8, -2.7, 13.0, 5.1)">
    <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
    <label anchor="(0,0)" alignment="sw" offset="(-3,-3)"><m>0</m></label>

    <!-- ================= trajectory (dashed parabola) ================= -->
    <graph at="pv-path" domain="(0,10.9)" function="f" stroke="black" thickness="1.5" dash="5"/>

    <!-- ================= acceleration a = g ================= -->
    <line at="pv-accel" p1="(4.2,0.55)" p2="(4.2,-0.55)" stroke="#f5a623" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
    <label anchor="(4.55,-0.5)" alignment="e"><m>\overrightarrow{a} = \overrightarrow{g} = -g\hat{\jmath}</m></label>

    <!-- ================= LAUNCH  P0 = (0,0), slope 1.4 ================= -->
    <line p1="(1.5,0)" p2="(1.5,2.1)" stroke="gray" thickness="1" dash="3"/>
    <line p1="(0,2.1)" p2="(1.5,2.1)" stroke="gray" thickness="1" dash="3"/>
