</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-intersection-collision">
  <title>*Intersection Points versus Collision Points</title>

  <p>
<em>Starred section.</em> This one is for the interested reader. It will not
be examined.
  </p>

  <p>
    A parametrization carries more information than the curve it traces: it also
    records <em>when</em> the moving point visits each place on the curve. Because
    of this, there are two different questions we can ask about two parametric
    curves
    <md>
      \mathbf r_1(t) = \bigl\langle f_1(t),\, g_1(t)\bigr\rangle, \qquad
      \mathbf r_2(t) = \bigl\langle f_2(t),\, g_2(t)\bigr\rangle,
    </md>
    thought of as the trajectories of two particles.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-intersection-collision">
    <title>Intersection Points and Collision Points</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A point <m>P</m> is an <term>intersection point</term> of the two curves if
        <m>P</m> lies on both curves; that is, if there are parameter values
        <m>t</m> and <m>s</m>, <em>not necessarily equal</em>, with
        <md xml:id="eq-intersection-condition" number="yes">
          \mathbf r_1(t) = \mathbf{OP} = \mathbf r_2(s).
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        A point <m>P</m> is a <term>collision point</term> of the two particles if
        both particles are at <m>P</m> at the <em>same</em> time; that is, if there
        is a single value of <m>t</m> with
        <md xml:id="eq-collision-condition" number="yes">
          \mathbf r_1(t) = \mathbf r_2(t) = \mathbf{OP}.
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    An intersection point is a statement about the two <em>paths</em>: they cross,
    like two roads on a map. A collision point is a statement about the two
    <em>motions</em>: the cars are at the crossroads at the same instant. Every
    collision point is automatically an intersection point, but, as the next two
    examples show, an intersection point need not be a collision point.
  </p>

  <p>
    In practice this dictates how we set up the equations.
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          To find <em>intersection</em> points, give the two curves
          <em>different</em> parameter names, as in
          <xref ref="eq-intersection-condition"/>, and solve
          <m>f_1(t) = f_2(s)</m>, <m>g_1(t) = g_2(s)</m> for the pair
          <m>(t,s)</m>. Equivalently, eliminate the parameters and intersect the
          two Cartesian curves.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          To find <em>collision</em> points, use the <em>same</em> parameter
          <m>t</m> in both curves and solve <m>f_1(t) = f_2(t)</m>,
          <m>g_1(t) = g_2(t)</m> for <m>t</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p>
    In the next two examples the two <em>paths</em> are always the same: the
    parabola <m>y = x^2</m> and the line <m>y = x+2</m>. Setting
    <m>x^2 = x+2</m> gives <m>x^2 - x - 2 = (x-2)(x+1) = 0</m>, so the paths meet
    at the two points
    <md>
      (-1,1) \qquad \text{and} \qquad (2,4).
    </md>
    Only the <em>schedule</em> of the second particle will change.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-no-collision">
    <title>The paths cross, but the particles never meet</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Two particles move in the plane with position vectors
        <md>
          \mathbf r_1(t) = \bigl\langle t,\; t^2\bigr\rangle, \qquad
          \mathbf r_2(t) = \bigl\langle -t,\; 2-t\bigr\rangle, \qquad t \in \mathbb R .
        </md>
        Find all intersection points of their paths, and all collision points of
        the particles.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Paths.</alert> Eliminating the parameter, the first particle traces
        the parabola <m>y = x^2</m>. For the second, <m>x = -t</m> and
        <m>y = 2-t = 2+x</m>, so it traces the line <m>y = x+2</m>. As computed
        above, the two paths intersect at <m>(-1,1)</m> and <m>(2,4)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Collisions.</alert> By <xref ref="eq-collision-condition"/>, a
        collision requires a single <m>t</m> with
        <md>
          t = -t \qquad \text{and} \qquad t^2 = 2 - t .
        </md>
        The first equation forces <m>t = 0</m>, but then <m>t^2 = 0</m> while
        <m>2 - t = 2</m>. There is no solution, so <em>the particles never
        collide</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        We can see exactly how they miss each other. The first particle is at
        <m>(-1,1)</m> when <m>t=-1</m>, while the second particle arrives there
        only at <m>t=1</m>. Similarly, the first particle reaches <m>(2,4)</m> at
        <m>t=2</m>, long after the second particle passed through it at
        <m>t=-2</m>. Each intersection point is visited twice, but never
        simultaneously. See <xref ref="fig-no-collision-video"/> and
        <xref ref="fig-no-collision"/>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <figure xml:id="fig-no-collision-video">
    <caption>
      The particles <m>\mathbf r_1(t) = \langle t,t^2\rangle</m> and
      <m>\mathbf r_2(t) = \langle -t,2-t\rangle</m> move simultaneously. Their paths cross at
      <m>(-1,1)</m> and <m>(2,4)</m>, but the clock shows that the two particles
      are never at either crossing at the same time.
    </caption>
    <video source="videos/no_collision_1080p.mp4" width="80%"/>
  </figure>

  <figure xml:id="fig-no-collision">
    <caption>
      The parabola <m>C_1</m> and the line <m>C_2</m> intersect at
      <m>(-1,1)</m> and <m>(2,4)</m>. At each of these points the two particles
      arrive at different times, so neither point is a collision point.
    </caption>
    <image width="70%">
      <description>
        <p>
          The parabola <m>y=x^2</m> and the line <m>y=x+2</m> drawn on the same
          axes, crossing at the points <m>(-1,1)</m> and <m>(2,4)</m>. Each
          crossing is labeled with the two different parameter values at which the
          two particles pass through it.
        </p>
      </description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="no-collision">
        <diagram dimensions="(340,340)" margins="5">

          <definition>r1(t) = (t, t^2)</definition>
          <definition>r2(t) = (-t, 2 - t)</definition>

          <coordinates bbox="(-4,-2,4,6)">
            <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>

            <parametric-curve at="path1" function="r1" domain="(-2.45,2.45)"
                              stroke="blue" thickness="2"/>
            <parametric-curve at="path2" function="r2" domain="(-4,4)"
                              stroke="red" thickness="2"/>

            <label at="label1" anchor="(2.6,5.6)" alignment="e" clear-background="no">
              <m>C_1</m>
            </label>
            <label at="label2" anchor="(-3.5,-1.2)" alignment="n" clear-background="no">
              <m>C_2</m>
            </label>

            <group at="pointA">
              <point p="(-1,1)" style="circle" fill="black"/>
              <label anchor="(-1.3,1)" alignment="nw" clear-background="no">
                <m>(-1,1)</m>
              </label>
              <!-- <label anchor="(-1,1)" alignment="sw" clear-background="no">
                <m>t=-1</m> vs. <m>t=1</m> 
              </label> -->
            </group>

            <group at="pointB">
              <point p="(2,4)" style="circle" fill="black"/>
              <label anchor="(2,4)" alignment="nw" clear-background="no">
                <m>(2,4)</m>
              </label>
             <!-- <label anchor="(2,4)" alignment="se" clear-background="no">
                <m>t=2</m> vs. <m>t=-2</m>
              </label> --> 
            </group>
          </coordinates>

          <annotations text="Two parametrized paths that intersect in two points, although the two particles never collide">
            <annotation ref="paths" text="The two paths">
              <annotation ref="path1" sonify="yes"
                          text="The path of the first particle, the parabola y equals x squared"
                          speech="The first particle moves along the parabola y equals x squared, with position r one of t equals t comma t squared"/>
              <annotation ref="path2" sonify="yes"
                          text="The path of the second particle, the line y equals x plus 2"
                          speech="The second particle moves along the line y equals x plus 2, with position r two of t equals negative t comma 2 minus t"/>
            </annotation>
            <annotation ref="intersections" text="The two intersection points of the paths">
              <annotation ref="pointA"
                          text="The intersection point negative 1 comma 1"
                          speech="The first particle is at the point negative 1 comma 1 when t equals negative 1, while the second particle is there when t equals 1. Since the times differ, this is not a collision."/>
              <annotation ref="pointB"
                          text="The intersection point 2 comma 4"
                          speech="The first particle is at the point 2 comma 4 when t equals 2, while the second particle is there when t equals negative 2. Since the times differ, this is not a collision."/>
            </annotation>
          </annotations>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <example xml:id="ex-collision">
    <title>The same paths, a different schedule<mdash/>now they collide</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Keep the first particle, but re-schedule the second one:
        <md>
          \mathbf r_1(t) = \bigl\langle t,\; t^2\bigr\rangle, \qquad
          \mathbf r_2(t) = \bigl\langle 2t-2,\; 2t\bigr\rangle, \qquad t \in \mathbb R .
        </md>
        Find the intersection points of the paths and the collision points of the
        particles.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Paths.</alert> For the second particle, <m>x = 2t-2</m> and
        <m>y = 2t = x+2</m>, so it travels along the very same line
        <m>y = x+2</m> as before, only faster and in the opposite direction. The
        paths are therefore unchanged, and they still intersect at <m>(-1,1)</m>
        and <m>(2,4)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Collisions.</alert> Now <xref ref="eq-collision-condition"/>
        requires a single <m>t</m> with
        <md>
          t = 2t - 2 \qquad \text{and} \qquad t^2 = 2t .
        </md>
        The first equation gives <m>t = 2</m>, and this value does satisfy the
        second equation, since <m>2^2 = 4 = 2\cdot 2</m>. Hence the particles
        <em>do</em> collide, at
        <md>
          \mathbf r_1(2) = \mathbf r_2(2) = \langle 2,4\rangle, \qquad \text{at time } t = 2 .
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The other intersection point is <em>not</em> a collision point: the first
        particle passes through <m>(-1,1)</m> at <m>t=-1</m>, while the second
        particle passes through it at <m>t = \tfrac12</m>. Two intersection
        points, but only one collision. See <xref ref="fig-collision-video"/> and
        <xref ref="fig-collision"/>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <figure xml:id="fig-collision-video">
    <caption>
      With <m>\mathbf r_2(t) = \langle 2t-2,2t\rangle</m> the second particle follows the same
      line as before, but on a different schedule. It misses the first particle at
      <m>(-1,1)</m> and collides with it at <m>(2,4)</m> when <m>t = 2</m>.
    </caption>
    <video source="videos/collision_1080p.mp4" width="80%"/>
  </figure>

  <figure xml:id="fig-collision">
    <caption>
      The same two paths as in <xref ref="fig-no-collision"/>. The point
      <m>(2,4)</m> is now a collision point, reached by both particles at
      <m>t=2</m>, while <m>(-1,1)</m> remains an intersection point that is not a
      collision point.
    </caption>
    <image width="70%">
      <description>
        <p>
          The parabola <m>y=x^2</m> and the line <m>y=x+2</m> crossing at
          <m>(-1,1)</m> and <m>(2,4)</m>. The point <m>(2,4)</m> is highlighted as
          a collision point, reached by both particles at time <m>t=2</m>, while
          the particles pass through <m>(-1,1)</m> at the different times
          <m>t=-1</m> and <m>t=\tfrac12</m>.
        </p>
      </description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="collision">
        <diagram dimensions="(340,340)" margins="5">

          <definition>r1(t) = (t, t^2)</definition>
          <definition>r2(t) = (2*t - 2, 2*t)</definition>

          <coordinates bbox="(-4,-2,4,6)">
            <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>

            <parametric-curve at="path1" function="r1" domain="(-2.45,2.45)"
                              stroke="blue" thickness="2"/>
            <parametric-curve at="path2" function="r2" domain="(-1,3)"
                              stroke="red" thickness="2"/>

            <label at="label1" anchor="(2.6,5.6)" alignment="e" clear-background="no">
              <m>C_1</m>
            </label>
            <label at="label2" anchor="(-3.5,-1.2)" alignment="n" clear-background="no">
              <m>C_2</m>
            </label>

            <group at="pointA">
              <point p="(-1,1)" style="circle" fill="black"/>
              <label anchor="(-1.2,1)" alignment="nw" clear-background="no">
                <m>(-1,1)</m>
              </label>
            <!-- <label anchor="(-1.5,1.2)" alignment="sw" clear-background="no">
                <m>t=-1</m> vs. <newline/> <m>t=\tfrac12</m>
              </label> -->
            </group>

            <group at="collision">
              <point p="(2,4)" style="circle" fill="green" size="6"/>
              <label anchor="(2,4)" alignment="nw" clear-background="no">
                <m>(2,4)</m>
              </label>
              <label anchor="(2,4)" alignment="se" clear-background="no">
                collision at <newline/>
                <m>t=2</m>
              </label>
            </group>
          </coordinates>

          <annotations text="Two parametrized paths that intersect in two points, exactly one of which is a collision">
            <annotation ref="paths" text="The two paths">
              <annotation ref="path1" sonify="yes"
                          text="The path of the first particle, the parabola y equals x squared"
                          speech="The first particle moves along the parabola y equals x squared, with position r one of t equals t comma t squared"/>
              <annotation ref="path2" sonify="yes"
                          text="The path of the second particle, the line y equals x plus 2"
                          speech="The second particle moves along the line y equals x plus 2, with position r two of t equals 2 t minus 2 comma 2 t"/>
            </annotation>
            <annotation ref="intersections" text="The two intersection points of the paths">
              <annotation ref="pointA"
                          text="The intersection point negative 1 comma 1, which is not a collision"
                          speech="The first particle is at the point negative 1 comma 1 when t equals negative 1, while the second particle is there when t equals one half. Since the times differ, this is not a collision."/>
              <annotation ref="collision"
                          text="The collision point 2 comma 4"
                          speech="Both particles are at the point 2 comma 4 at the same time, t equals 2. This intersection point is a collision point."/>
            </annotation>
          </annotations>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <insight xml:id="insight-collision-vs-intersection">
    <title>Every Collision Is an Intersection, but Not Conversely</title>
          <p>
        If two particles collide at <m>P</m>, then certainly <m>P</m> lies on both
        paths, so every collision point is an intersection point. The converse
        fails: the paths may cross at a point that the two particles visit at
        different times. Changing the parametrization of a curve does not move the
        curve, but it can create or destroy collisions.
      </p>
  </insight>

  <exercise xml:id="checkpoint-collision">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Keep <m>\mathbf r_1(t) = \langle t,t^2\rangle</m>. Find a parametrization
        <m>\mathbf r_2</m> of the line <m>y = x+2</m> for which the two particles
        collide at <em>both</em> intersection points.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Try to arrange that the second particle has the same <m>x</m>-coordinate as
        the first one at every time <m>t</m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\mathbf r_2(t) = \langle t,\; t+2\rangle</m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>
        With <m>\mathbf r_2(t) = \langle t, t+2\rangle</m> the collision equations become
        <m>t = t</m> and <m>t^2 = t+2</m>, i.e. <m>(t-2)(t+1) = 0</m>. Thus
        <m>t = -1</m> gives the collision at <m>(-1,1)</m> and <m>t = 2</m> gives
        the collision at <m>(2,4)</m>: both intersection points are now collision
        points.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

</subsection>



</section>
